1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:08,118
Orchard Core keeps its content, users, settings and workflows in a relational database, through YesSql.

2
00:00:08,118 --> 00:00:12,690
In this video you see how documents and indexes work, how a site

3
00:00:12,690 --> 00:00:18,110
chooses its database, which options tune YesSql, and how to run your own SQL.

4
00:00:22,710 --> 00:00:25,963
Orchard Core doesn't use Entity Framework.

5
00:00:25,963 --> 00:00:31,926
It stores its content items, users, settings and workflows with YesSql, a document

6
00:00:31,926 --> 00:00:35,450
database interface over a relational database.

7
00:00:36,200 --> 00:00:40,820
When you save an object, YesSql serializes it to JSON and stores it

8
00:00:40,820 --> 00:00:43,035
as a row in a document table.

9
00:00:43,035 --> 00:00:48,920
The default table is named Document, and its columns are Id, Type, Content and Version.

10
00:00:49,670 --> 00:00:55,304
Because the content is JSON, there is no schema to migrate when your objects change.

11
00:00:55,304 --> 00:01:00,250
Adding a field or a part to a content type leaves the tables as they are.

12
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:06,040
SQL can't efficiently query inside the JSON, and that is what indexes are for.

13
00:01:06,040 --> 00:01:10,960
An index is a plain C# class that holds the properties you want to query on.

14
00:01:11,710 --> 00:01:16,875
YesSql stores index data in regular SQL tables, and recomputes the rows in

15
00:01:16,875 --> 00:01:21,910
the same transaction whenever a document is created, updated or deleted.

16
00:01:22,660 --> 00:01:26,037
In a map index, each row maps to one document.

17
00:01:26,037 --> 00:01:31,817
ContentItemIndex maps every content item version to its content type, whether it is

18
00:01:31,817 --> 00:01:34,154
published, its owner and more.

19
00:01:34,154 --> 00:01:38,960
A reduce index aggregates a group of documents, like a SQL GROUP BY.

20
00:01:43,160 --> 00:01:47,738
You read and write documents through ISession, a scoped unit of work.

21
00:01:47,738 --> 00:01:50,790
SaveAsync and Delete are buffered in memory.

22
00:01:50,790 --> 00:01:56,100
SaveChangesAsync flushes the changes to the database and commits the transaction.

23
00:01:56,850 --> 00:02:01,317
Orchard Core commits the session of a tenant's shell scope, the scope of

24
00:02:01,317 --> 00:02:05,383
one site, when that scope ends, normally at the end of a request.

25
00:02:05,383 --> 00:02:08,647
An exception leaving the scope cancels the changes.

26
00:02:08,647 --> 00:02:12,140
A session from any other scope isn't committed for you.

27
00:02:12,890 --> 00:02:15,851
To read documents, filter on an index.

28
00:02:15,851 --> 00:02:20,832
This query asks for the published content items of one content type.

29
00:02:20,832 --> 00:02:25,543
YesSql runs the filter as SQL on the index table, then loads and

30
00:02:25,543 --> 00:02:28,370
deserializes the matching documents.

31
00:02:29,120 --> 00:02:34,948
For content items, prefer IContentManager, which handles loading, versions and handlers.

32
00:02:34,948 --> 00:02:40,590
Use the session to query your own indexes, which a separate video shows how to write.

33
00:02:44,790 --> 00:02:50,277
Each tenant, one site of the application, uses one of four database providers.

34
00:02:50,277 --> 00:02:54,130
SQLite is the default and needs no connection string.

35
00:02:54,130 --> 00:02:59,552
SQL Server, MySQL and PostgreSQL need a connection string, and accept a table

36
00:02:59,552 --> 00:03:01,250
prefix and a schema.

37
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:07,321
For SQL Server, MySQL and PostgreSQL, create the database before setup, and give

38
00:03:07,321 --> 00:03:10,848
the account permission to create and modify tables.

39
00:03:10,848 --> 00:03:15,117
Orchard Core validates the connection, then creates its tables.

40
00:03:15,117 --> 00:03:17,840
It doesn't create the database itself.

41
00:03:19,516 --> 00:03:22,990
The Setup screen asks What type of database to use?

42
00:03:22,990 --> 00:03:27,986
Sqlite is selected, and it needs no connection string, so none is asked for.

43
00:03:28,483 --> 00:03:30,230
Let's choose Sql Server.

44
00:03:30,230 --> 00:03:31,744
Three fields appear:

45
00:03:31,744 --> 00:03:34,947
Table Prefix, Connection String and Table Schema.

46
00:03:34,947 --> 00:03:39,023
A hint under Connection String shows a sample for that provider.

47
00:03:39,501 --> 00:03:43,117
The table prefix lets several sites share one database.

48
00:03:43,117 --> 00:03:46,910
Leave Table Schema blank to use the default on the server.

49
00:03:46,910 --> 00:03:50,881
The connection string is masked, and the eye button shows it.

50
00:03:52,481 --> 00:03:57,056
The provider, connection string, table prefix and schema are tenant settings.

51
00:03:57,056 --> 00:04:01,465
Enter them on the Setup screen, or set them in the tenant's configuration.

52
00:04:01,465 --> 00:04:06,591
When the provider and connection string are set, the Setup screen doesn't ask for them.

53
00:04:10,791 --> 00:04:15,661
SQLite stores each tenant's database as a file in the tenant's folder.

54
00:04:15,661 --> 00:04:22,261
The DatabaseName setting chooses the file name, and Setup uses OrchardCore.db when none is given.

55
00:04:23,011 --> 00:04:27,837
The SQLite provider pools connections by default, and a pooled connection can keep

56
00:04:27,837 --> 00:04:31,675
the file open, which may interfere with copying it for a backup.

57
00:04:31,675 --> 00:04:34,965
Set UseConnectionPooling to false to release the file.

58
00:04:34,965 --> 00:04:37,651
Turning pooling off can reduce performance.

59
00:04:41,851 --> 00:04:46,947
Orchard Core binds the YesSql section of the configuration to YesSqlOptions.

60
00:04:46,947 --> 00:04:49,682
Any tenant configuration source works.

61
00:04:49,682 --> 00:04:54,591
In a tenant's own settings file, leave out the outer OrchardCore section.

62
00:04:55,341 --> 00:05:02,187
CommandsPageSize, 500 by default, caps the commands in one page, and QueryGatingEnabled makes

63
00:05:02,187 --> 00:05:05,230
identical concurrent queries run once.

64
00:05:05,230 --> 00:05:11,591
IsolationLevel sets the default transaction isolation level, ReadCommitted by default.

65
00:05:12,341 --> 00:05:18,455
ConcurrentIndexCreationEnabled, true by default, lets a data migration add an index to an

66
00:05:18,455 --> 00:05:21,351
existing table without blocking writes.

67
00:05:21,351 --> 00:05:26,306
That works online on SQL Server editions that support it, and on MySQL.

68
00:05:26,306 --> 00:05:28,301
PostgreSQL blocks writes.

69
00:05:29,051 --> 00:05:36,396
IncludeQueryTextInTelemetry, off by default, adds the SQL text to the telemetry spans of database commands.

70
00:05:36,396 --> 00:05:41,076
Raw SQL fragments are included as they are, so the text can expose

71
00:05:41,076 --> 00:05:44,001
data to whoever receives the telemetry.

72
00:05:48,201 --> 00:05:53,956
TableOptions defines presets that Orchard Core copies into a tenant's settings during setup.

73
00:05:53,956 --> 00:06:00,826
DefaultDocumentTable names the document table, and DefaultTableNameSeparator joins a table prefix or collection

74
00:06:00,826 --> 00:06:02,411
name to a table name.

75
00:06:03,161 --> 00:06:07,414
The separator is one or more underscores, or NULL for none.

76
00:06:07,414 --> 00:06:11,471
DefaultIdentityColumnSize is Int64 by default, or Int32.

77
00:06:11,471 --> 00:06:15,070
Configure the presets before you set up a tenant:

78
00:06:15,070 --> 00:06:19,911
changing them later doesn't rename tables or alter identity columns.

79
00:06:24,111 --> 00:06:27,759
For tables of your own, create them in a data migration.

80
00:06:27,759 --> 00:06:33,700
At runtime, IDbConnectionAccessor creates a connection for the current tenant, and IStore gives

81
00:06:33,700 --> 00:06:37,171
you the SQL dialect, the schema and the table prefix.

82
00:06:37,921 --> 00:06:43,127
Database providers quote identifiers differently, so build the table name from the store

83
00:06:43,127 --> 00:06:45,951
configuration, and quote it with the dialect.

84
00:06:45,951 --> 00:06:50,769
The table prefix already includes the separator, and passing the schema keeps the

85
00:06:50,769 --> 00:06:53,261
query working for tenants that use one.

86
00:06:54,011 --> 00:06:59,538
Table names can't be SQL parameters, so compose them only from trusted configuration,

87
00:06:59,538 --> 00:07:02,028
and pass data values as parameters.

88
00:07:02,028 --> 00:07:06,947
For related writes, begin a transaction, pass it to every command, and roll

89
00:07:06,947 --> 00:07:09,011
back and rethrow on failure.

90
00:07:11,011 --> 00:07:13,516
That is how Orchard Core stores data:

91
00:07:13,516 --> 00:07:18,758
documents and indexes in a relational database, a provider that each tenant chooses,

92
00:07:18,758 --> 00:07:20,797
and options that tune YesSql.

93
00:07:20,797 --> 00:07:22,311
Thanks for watching.

